---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const APP = "https://login.form4dev.com";
const title = "Forminit alternatives in 2026 (formerly Getform): an honest comparison";
const description =
  "Getform rebranded to Forminit in early 2026. If you're evaluating alternatives — or considering moving off Forminit — here are the form backends worth knowing about, with honest tradeoffs on price, features, and fit.";
const publishedAt = "2026-05-16";
const updatedAt = "2026-05-16";

const fmtDate = (iso: string) =>
  new Date(iso + "T00:00:00Z").toLocaleDateString("en-US", {
    year: "numeric",
    month: "long",
    day: "numeric",
  });
---

<Base title={`${title} — Form4Dev`} description={description} current="blog">
  <article class="post container-wide">
    <header class="post-head">
      <a href="/blog" class="back mono">← Blog</a>
      <div class="post-meta mono">
        <time datetime={publishedAt}>{fmtDate(publishedAt)}</time>
        <span class="dot">·</span>
        <span>11 min read</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="display">{title}</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Getform rebranded to Forminit in early 2026. If you've landed here
        looking for alternatives — or you're on Forminit and wondering
        what else is out there — this is a straight comparison of the
        form backends worth considering, with the tradeoffs each one
        actually carries.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="body">
      <h2>Quick context: what changed with the rebrand</h2>
      <p>
        Getform — a long-running form-backend service popular with static
        site builders — rebranded to Forminit in January 2026. The
        product, endpoints, and pricing largely carried over; the name and
        the marketing positioning didn't. If you've been on Getform for
        years, your forms still work. If you're shopping for a form
        backend today and seeing "Forminit" everywhere, that's why.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rebrand is a reasonable moment to take stock. Form backends
        are easy to switch — the only real lock-in is the endpoint URL —
        and the category has gotten more competitive in the last 18
        months. Below is what's worth comparing, written by someone who
        builds in this space and reads the competitor landing pages
        weekly.
      </p>

      <h2>Disclosure</h2>
      <p>
        I build <a href="https://form4dev.com">Form4Dev</a>, one of the
        services listed here. I've tried to write this fairly — naming
        where competitors genuinely win, including the cases where I'd
        recommend them over my own product. If something below feels
        slanted, email me and I'll fix it.
      </p>

      <h2>How to think about picking one</h2>
      <p>
        Form backends compete on a small number of axes. The right one for
        you depends on which axis you weigh hardest.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Free-tier generosity</strong> — how many submissions per
          month you get without paying, and whether features (webhooks,
          exports, integrations) are gated behind paid plans.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Dashboard and UX</strong> — how nice the inbox is to live
          in, especially if you'll spend time reviewing submissions.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Developer ergonomics</strong> — clear API, OpenAPI spec,
          predictable error envelopes, CLI or library support.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Programmatic submission</strong> — whether non-browser
          clients (scripts, mobile apps, AI agents) can submit cleanly.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Email deliverability</strong> — notifications actually
          landing in your inbox, with Reply-To routed sensibly.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Price at scale</strong> — what you'll pay at 10k or
          100k submissions per month, including any per-submission
          overages.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Self-hosting</strong> — whether you can run it on your
          own infrastructure, if that matters to you.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        No service wins on all of these. Pick the one that wins on the
        axes that hurt most.
      </p>

      <h2>The alternatives, ranked by fit</h2>

      <h3>1. Formspree — the incumbent</h3>
      <p>
        Formspree is the oldest meaningful player in the category. They've
        been doing this since 2014, have the most integrations, the most
        documentation, the best React library (<code>@formspree/react</code>),
        and the most third-party tutorials. If you Google "how to add a
        contact form to my Astro site," you'll find a Formspree tutorial.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Formspree wins</strong>: maturity, framework
        libraries, integration breadth (Slack, Mailchimp, Stripe, Zapier,
        AWS, Webhooks, the lot), and the world's deepest documentation
        corpus. If you want the "boring choice that just works," this is
        it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Formspree loses</strong>: pricing climbs fast above
        the free tier (50 submissions/month on free), the dashboard
        aesthetic feels like it was designed in 2017, and there's no
        first-class Bearer-token authentication for non-browser clients.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pick Formspree if</strong>: you want the most mature,
        most-documented option and don't mind paying for it past 50
        submissions a month.
      </p>

      <h3>2. Basin — the design-forward option</h3>
      <p>
        Basin (<code>usebasin.com</code>) positions explicitly toward
        designers and "build forms that work, without all the work." The
        dashboard is the prettiest in the category by some distance, the
        onboarding flow is excellent, and they've gone deep on visual
        polish in places competitors don't bother.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Basin wins</strong>: hands-down the nicest UI to
        live in, strong spam filtering out of the box, generous free tier
        (100 submissions/month with most features), $8.25/mo starter
        plan that's competitive.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Basin loses</strong>: developer ergonomics are a
        secondary concern — there's no OpenAPI spec, the docs lean more
        on screenshots than code, and programmatic submission is a
        second-class path.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pick Basin if</strong>: you'll spend real time in the
        dashboard, you care about how it looks when you demo to a client,
        and your forms are filled by humans in browsers.
      </p>

      <h3>3. Formcarry — the lifetime-archive option</h3>
      <p>
        Formcarry's pricing starts at $6/mo, but the differentiator is the
        lifetime submission archive on free. Most competitors cap free
        tier submissions or rotate them out; Formcarry keeps them.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Formcarry wins</strong>: cheapest paid plan in the
        category, lifetime archive on free tier, CSV and JSON export with
        no plan gating, file uploads on most plans.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Formcarry loses</strong>: the product surface is
        thinner — fewer integrations, less polish, no published OpenAPI
        spec — and the documentation is closer to "reference" than
        "tutorial."
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pick Formcarry if</strong>: price is the dominant axis and
        you want submissions archived forever without paying for retention.
      </p>

      <h3>4. Forminit (formerly Getform) — the static-site veteran</h3>
      <p>
        Getform-now-Forminit has been around for years and has a strong
        following in the Hugo/Eleventy/Jekyll communities. The rebrand
        modernized the marketing site and tightened the positioning; the
        underlying product is the same well-tested form backend it's
        always been.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Forminit wins</strong>: rock-solid reliability
        (years of operation behind it), strong static-site-generator
        integration guides, good Zapier and webhook support, file upload
        support on all paid plans.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Forminit loses</strong>: pricing is mid-pack ($9/mo
        starter, climbing to $24+), the dashboard is functional rather
        than delightful, no first-class agent or Bearer-token primitive.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pick Forminit if</strong>: you're already on Getform and
        the rebrand made you nervous — there's no reason to leave just
        because the name changed. The product is the same.
      </p>

      <h3>5. Web3Forms — the unlimited-free option</h3>
      <p>
        Web3Forms takes a different tack: unlimited submissions on the
        free tier, with most features open, monetizing via paid tiers that
        unlock dashboards and team features. If your form-submission
        volume is unpredictable and you don't want to think about quota,
        Web3Forms removes the worry.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Web3Forms wins</strong>: unlimited free submissions,
        simple pricing, fast setup, good for prototypes and side projects.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Web3Forms loses</strong>: no dashboard on free tier
        (you only get email notifications), less battle-tested
        deliverability than Formspree-class services, integration depth is
        thinner.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pick Web3Forms if</strong>: you want a free form backend
        for a personal site or prototype and the email-only-no-dashboard
        flow is fine for you.
      </p>

      <h3>6. Netlify Forms — the platform-native option</h3>
      <p>
        Netlify Forms is built into Netlify's hosting platform. If your
        site is already on Netlify, you can wire up a form by adding a
        single attribute to your HTML — no third-party service to sign up
        for.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Netlify Forms wins</strong>: zero-config when your
        site is on Netlify, included in their plans up to a quota, native
        integrations with Netlify Functions for custom logic.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Netlify Forms loses</strong>: locked to Netlify
        hosting (move and you rewrite your forms), and recent changes to
        credit-based billing have made the cost less predictable. Not an
        option if you're on Vercel, Cloudflare Pages, or anywhere else.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pick Netlify Forms if</strong>: you're already on Netlify
        and don't anticipate moving, and the included quota covers your
        volume.
      </p>

      <h3>7. Formspark — the lifetime-deal option</h3>
      <p>
        Formspark is the budget option with a different model: you pay
        once for a pool of submissions and use them at your own pace, no
        monthly subscription. A $25 one-time payment buys 50,000
        submissions you can use over years.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Formspark wins</strong>: no recurring cost, simple
        mental model, great for low-traffic sites where a subscription
        feels overweight.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Formspark loses</strong>: feature surface is
        intentionally minimal, no first-class team features, less suited
        to anyone running multiple production sites.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pick Formspark if</strong>: you have one or two
        low-traffic sites and the idea of paying once for a multi-year
        submission budget appeals.
      </p>

      <h3>8. Form4Dev — the agent-first option</h3>
      <p>
        Full disclosure (again): this is my product. I'll keep it short
        and let you evaluate.
      </p>
      <p>
        Form4Dev is a form backend designed from day one with non-browser
        submitters as first-class clients. The differentiators are
        Bearer-token authentication scoped per-account, an OpenAPI 3.1
        spec at a public URL (so agent frameworks can consume the schema
        directly), per-tenant SMTP for organizations that want
        notifications sent from their own domain, and transparent pricing
        ($0 / $9 / $29) with no per-feature gating between tiers.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Form4Dev wins</strong>: cleanest path for agent and
        scripted submission, published OpenAPI spec, per-tenant SMTP on
        every paid plan, transparent flat pricing, every feature available
        on free tier (just at lower quota).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where Form4Dev loses</strong>: we're early. We don't have
        Formspree's decade of integrations, Basin's dashboard polish, or
        Forminit's static-site-generator tutorial corpus. The product is
        production-ready; the surrounding ecosystem is younger.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pick Form4Dev if</strong>: you're submitting forms from
        scripts, mobile apps, or AI agents; you value transparent flat
        pricing over per-tier feature gating; you read OpenAPI specs for
        fun.
      </p>

      <h2>Side-by-side at a glance</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Service</th>
            <th>Free tier</th>
            <th>Starter</th>
            <th>Best for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Formspree</td>
            <td>50/mo</td>
            <td>$10/mo</td>
            <td>Maturity, integrations</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Basin</td>
            <td>100/mo</td>
            <td>$8.25/mo</td>
            <td>UI quality</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Formcarry</td>
            <td>100/mo (lifetime archive)</td>
            <td>$6/mo</td>
            <td>Price + archive</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Forminit</td>
            <td>50/mo</td>
            <td>$9/mo</td>
            <td>Static-site reliability</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Web3Forms</td>
            <td>Unlimited</td>
            <td>$10/mo (dashboard)</td>
            <td>Free unlimited</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Netlify Forms</td>
            <td>Included on plan</td>
            <td>Tied to hosting</td>
            <td>Netlify-hosted sites</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Formspark</td>
            <td>—</td>
            <td>$25 one-time / 50k</td>
            <td>No subscription</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Form4Dev</td>
            <td>100/mo, all features</td>
            <td>$9/mo</td>
            <td>Agents, scripts, OpenAPI</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted-note">
        Pricing accurate as of {fmtDate(updatedAt)} based on each
        service's public pricing page. Free-tier limits are the headline
        number — most services include or exclude features at the free
        tier in ways that matter; check the linked pricing pages before
        committing.
      </p>

      <h2>How to actually migrate</h2>
      <p>
        Form backends are easy to switch. The work is mostly clerical.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Export your existing submissions</strong>. Every service
          on this list supports CSV export of historical submissions. Do
          this first, before changing anything — even if you don't need
          the data, you want it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sign up for the new service and create your forms</strong>.
          Match field names so your downstream automations (webhooks,
          CRMs, Slack notifications) don't break.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Update the <code>action=</code> URL on every form</strong>.
          If you used an environment variable or a single source-of-truth
          constant, this is one change. If you hardcoded the endpoint in
          50 templates, this is 50 changes — keep a list.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Test in a staging environment</strong>. Submit each
          form once, verify the notification arrives, verify webhooks
          fire, verify the data lands.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ship</strong>. Leave the old service active for a week
          in case anything routes there. Then revoke its keys.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Total time for most sites: under an afternoon.
      </p>

      <h2>FAQ</h2>

      <h3>Is Forminit the same as Getform?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes. Getform rebranded to Forminit in January 2026. Existing
        endpoints, accounts, and submissions carried over. If you're an
        existing Getform customer, you don't need to do anything — your
        forms still work.
      </p>

      <h3>What's the best free form backend in 2026?</h3>
      <p>
        "Best" depends on what you weigh. Web3Forms wins on raw quota
        (unlimited submissions). Basin wins on free-tier UX. Form4Dev
        gives you all features on free, capped at 100 submissions/month.
        Formspree is the safest "boring choice" with 50/mo. Pick based on
        the axis that matters to your use case, not the headline number.
      </p>

      <h3>Which form backend is best for AI agents?</h3>
      <p>
        Of the services here, Form4Dev is the only one with first-class
        Bearer-token authentication scoped per-account and a published
        OpenAPI 3.1 spec built for agent consumption. The others can
        accept agent submissions, but you'll be fighting their spam
        defenses. We wrote about this in more depth in
        <a href="/blog/form-backend-for-ai-agents">our post on form
        endpoints for AI agents</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Which form backend has the best deliverability?</h3>
      <p>
        Formspree, Basin, and Forminit are all mature on this axis —
        years of operation, established sending infrastructure, good
        Reply-To routing. Newer services (including ours) are catching
        up; if you're sending notifications to strict corporate inboxes,
        check whichever service you pick by sending a test submission to
        the destination before committing.
      </p>

      <h3>Can I self-host any of these?</h3>
      <p>
        None of the services listed offer first-party self-hosting at
        time of writing. There are open-source form-backend projects
        (FormGrid is the most active), but they require running your own
        infrastructure. If self-hosting is a hard requirement, evaluate
        those separately.
      </p>

      <h3>How much will I really pay at 10,000 submissions a month?</h3>
      <p>
        Roughly: Formspree $35-50/mo, Basin $25/mo, Formcarry $15-30/mo,
        Forminit $24/mo, Web3Forms $10/mo (with dashboard), Form4Dev
        $29/mo (Team plan, no per-submission overage up to its cap).
        Verify each on the current pricing page — pricing changes more
        often than blog posts get updated.
      </p>

      <h2>The takeaway</h2>
      <p>
        The form-backend category isn't a zero-sum fight. Different
        services optimize for different users — designers, static-site
        builders, side projects, enterprise teams, agent operators. The
        Forminit rebrand is a useful moment to look around, but most
        people land back where they started after looking, which is also
        a valid outcome.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you're moving for a specific reason — pricing surprise, a
        feature gap, an integration that broke — name the reason
        explicitly before you shop. That's how you avoid switching just
        to switch.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the agent-first angle is what brought you here,
        <a href="https://form4dev.com">Form4Dev</a> is built around it
        and the free tier includes Bearer-token auth.
        <a href={`${APP}/register`} class="plausible-event-name=Signup+CTA plausible-event-location=blog-forminit-alts plausible-event-plan=free">
          Start free
        </a>
        — no credit card, all features unlocked, capped at 100
        submissions/month. Try it on a real form, see if it fits.
      </p>

      <p class="muted-note last-updated">
        Last updated {fmtDate(updatedAt)}. Pricing and features change —
        spotted something out of date? Email
        <a href="mailto:hello@form4dev.com">hello@form4dev.com</a> and
        I'll fix it.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

